<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John of Bohemia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1296">1296</a>–<a href="/years/1346">1346</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as John the Blind, John of Luxembourg · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-luxembourg">House of Luxembourg</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, known as John the Blind, was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-of-bohemia">King of Bohemia</a> from 1310 to 1346. Son of Emperor Henry VII, he gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> by marriage to Elisabeth of the Přemyslid line and spent much of his reign campaigning abroad. Between 1327 and 1336 most of the Silesian dukes did homage to him, bringing Silesia under the Bohemian crown. Blind by then, he was killed fighting for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-crecy">Crécy</a> in 1346.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-of-bohemia.jpg" alt="John of Bohemia" width="320" height="261" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Packare · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_John_the_Blind_(died_1346),_Count_of_Luxembourg,_King_of_Bohemia,_St_Vitus_Cathedral,_Prague.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John was the eldest son of Emperor Henry VII and Margaret of Brabant. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> and raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, he was French by education but deeply involved in the politics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. In 1310 his father arranged his marriage, at the age of fourteen, to Elizabeth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>. The couple travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> under the protection of imperial regiments.</p><p>Czech forces took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> and deposed the reigning king, Henry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>, on 3 December 1310. John and Elizabeth were crowned on 7 February 1311. His attempt to follow his father as King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> failed when Louis IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> was elected in 1314.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Much of the Czech nobility disliked John as an alien king. In 1311 he reached an agreement with the Bohemian and Moravian aristocracy, the so-called inaugural diplomas, which kept the right to elect the king and to decide extraordinary taxation with the nobles and abolished the king's right to appoint foreign officials. The settlement did not hold, and John, who preferred foreign politics, gave up the administration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and travelled, leaving the country to be ruled by the barons. By 1318 he had reconciled with the nobility and recognised their rights.</p><p>Abroad he served his father as Imperial Vicar and pressed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin">House of Wettin</a> for territory. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-ascania">House of Ascania</a> died out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> he regained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bautzen">Bautzen</a> in 1319 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorlitz">Görlitz</a> in 1329. He supported Louis IV against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-fair">Frederick the Fair</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muhldorf">Battle of Mühldorf</a> in 1322 and received the region of Egerland as a reward, but allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> against Louis in 1322 or 1323. In 1335, at the Congress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visegrad">Visegrád</a>, Casimir III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> paid a large sum for John to give up his claim to the Polish throne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>John lost his sight to ophthalmia while crusading in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a> began in 1337 he allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-vi-of-france">Philip VI of France</a> and served as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a> from 30 November 1338 to November 1340. On 26 August 1346 he commanded the French vanguard at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-crecy">Battle of Crécy</a> and was killed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-froissart">Jean Froissart</a> records that his companions tied their bridles together and led him into the fight.</p><p>His eldest son Charles succeeded him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and his son Wenceslaus, by his second wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beatrice-of-bourbon">Beatrice of Bourbon</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>. His remains were placed in the crypt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/notre-dame-de-paris">Notre-Dame Cathedral</a>, Luxembourg, in 1945. He is regarded as a national hero in Luxembourg.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John of Bohemia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Count Henry VII became emperor in 1308, and his son <b>John the Blind</b>, king of Bohemia, died at Crécy in 1346.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-luxembourg">County of Luxembourg</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1329 Bolesław did homage to <b>King John of Bohemia</b>, placing Brzeg under the Kingdom of Bohemia.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-brzeg">Duchy of Brzeg</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1327 its duke did homage to <b>King John of Bohemia</b>, making the duchy a fief of the Kingdom of Bohemia.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-kozle">Duchy of Koźle</a></li><li><blockquote>He was close to the Bohemian court and in 1327 did homage to <b>King John of Bohemia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-niemodlin">Duchy of Niemodlin</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1329 he did homage to <b>King John of Bohemia</b>, placing the duchy under the Kingdom of Bohemia.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-olesnica">Duchy of Oleśnica</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1327 his successor did homage to <b>John of Bohemia</b>, making the duchy a fief of the Kingdom of Bohemia.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-oswiecim">Duchy of Oswiecim</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Luxembourg. Died in Crécy-en-Ponthieu, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.61,6.13&amp;year=1328" aria-label="Luxembourg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4691 436 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John of Bohemia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5141 706h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5141 706h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.61,6.13&amp;year=1328">What was here in 1328? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/henry-vii-holy-roman-emperor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/henry-vii-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Henry VII, Holy Roman Emperor</span><span class="tp">Father &#183; c. 1275–1313</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/john-henry-margrave-of-moravia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/john-henry-margrave-of-moravia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">John Henry, Margrave of Moravia</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; 1322–1375</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-i-duke-of-luxembourg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/wenceslaus-i-duke-of-luxembourg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Wenceslaus I, Duke of Luxembourg</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; 1337–1383</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I require you bring me so far forward, that I may strike one stroke with my sword.&#8221;</p><footer>Words to his knights at Crécy, 1346, where he died blind (Froissart, Chronicles) &#183; tr. John Bourchier, Lord Berners, 1525 &#183; Froissart's account &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/basis/froissart-full.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
